[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-010-event-bus-communication-pattern.md#variation-1-event-bus-with-replay-buffer]

#### Variation 1: Event Bus with Replay Buffer

**Use Case**: Late-subscribing modules need to process events that occurred before they mounted.

**Implementation:**

```typescript
// shell/src/services/EventBusWithReplay.ts
export class EventBusWithReplay extends EventBus {
  private replayBuffer = new Map<string, any[]>();
  private bufferSize = 10;  // Keep last 10 events per type

  publish<T>(eventType: string, data: T, schema: z.ZodSchema<T>): void {
    super.publish(eventType, data, schema);

    // Add to replay buffer
    if (!this.replayBuffer.has(eventType)) {
      this.replayBuffer.set(eventType, []);
    }

    const buffer = this.replayBuffer.get(eventType)!;
    buffer.push(data);

    // Keep only last N events
    if (buffer.length > this.bufferSize) {
      buffer.shift();
    }
  }

  subscribe<T>(eventType: string, handler: EventHandler<T>, replay = false): () => void {
    // If replay=true, invoke handler with buffered events
    if (replay && this.replayBuffer.has(eventType)) {
      const bufferedEvents = this.replayBuffer.get(eventType)!;
      bufferedEvents.forEach(event => handler(event));
    }

    return super.subscribe(eventType, handler);
  }
}
```

**Trade-offs:**
- ✅ Late-subscribing modules don't miss critical events
- ✅ Useful for analytics modules that mount after user actions
- ❌ Increased memory usage (buffering events)
- ❌ Subscribers must be idempotent (may process same event twice)

