[patterns/application/micro-frontend/PTN-APP-MFE-010-event-bus-communication-pattern.md#variation-3-event-bus-with-backend-integration]

#### Variation 3: Event Bus with Backend Integration

**Use Case**: Frontend events should also be published to backend event bus (EventBridge, Kafka) for durability.

**Implementation:**

```typescript
export class EventBusWithBackend extends EventBus {
  constructor(private backendEventService: BackendEventService) {
    super();
  }

  publish<T>(eventType: string, data: T, schema: z.ZodSchema<T>): void {
    // Publish to frontend subscribers
    super.publish(eventType, data, schema);

    // Also publish to backend event bus (fire-and-forget)
    this.backendEventService.publish(eventType, data).catch(error => {
      console.error('Failed to publish event to backend:', error);
      Sentry.captureException(error, { extra: { eventType, data } });
    });
  }
}

// Backend event service
class BackendEventService {
  async publish(eventType: string, data: any): Promise<void> {
    await fetch('/api/events', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ eventType, data, timestamp: new Date().toISOString() }),
    });
  }
}
```

**Trade-offs:**
- ✅ Frontend events persisted to backend for durability
- ✅ Backend systems can react to frontend events
- ✅ Unified event stream across frontend and backend
- ❌ Network overhead for every event
- ❌ Potential for duplicate events (frontend + backend subscribers)

